<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burma, now Myanmar, is a country in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> fought a series of costly and unsuccessful wars against Burma in the 1760s. Burma was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> in the nineteenth century, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1942, and became independent in 1948 during British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burma.jpg" alt="Burma" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Myanmar.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Court factionalism then opened the door to <b>Burma</b>: the dismissed Borphukan Badan Chandra sought help from the Konbaung kings, and Burmese armies entered Assam in 1817, returning in 1819 and 1821, when they made the valley a province of Burma.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom's long wars were fought first against Sukhothai, Angkor and the northern kingdom of Lan Na, and then above all against <b>Burma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…August 1945, and the Anglo-American loan negotiated to replace it was not finally repaid until 2006, while Japan's rapid conquest of Hong Kong, Malaya, Singapore and <b>Burma</b> in 1941–1942 — the fall of Singapore in February 1942 above all — shattered the myth of British invincibility in Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Second World War the Indian Army grew to about 2.5 million men, often called the largest volunteer army in history, and helped defeat Japan in <b>Burma</b> after the battles of Imphal and Kohima in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gold Coast Regiment fought for Britain against German forces in Cameroon and in the long East African campaign of 1914–18, and in the Second World War Gold Coast troops served in Ethiopia and <b>Burma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Konbaung armies combined ahmudan regiments, provincial levies and Shan contingents with foreign specialists, including French gunners captured at Syriam in 1756, and they were capable of long campaigns far beyond <b>Burma</b>’s borders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
